.TH DESKTOP 1 "" "hibr @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
desktop \- hibr's text-mode desktop, held so it can always be detached from
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B desktop
.RB [ \-r | \-\-resume ]
.RB [ \-s | \-\-session
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-j | \-\-join ]
.RB [ \-w | \-\-standby ]
.RB [ \-n | \-\-name
.IR name ]
.br
.B desktop ctl
.RB [ \-s | \-\-session
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-S | \-\-socket
.IR path ]
.I request
.RI [ arg ...]
.br
.B desktop ctl
.B \-j
.I json
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B desktop
starts hibr's desktop in the terminal it is run from: windows, a menu bar,
desktop icons and a Control Panel, drawn in the terminal's own cells and,
where the terminal can show them, in pixels. It is a hibr script
.RI ( session.hibr
in the desktop's folder), run by
.BR hibr (1).
.PP
Every desktop is
.IR held :
it runs inside a session of hibr's
.B hold
module, so it outlives the terminal. Detach, from the hibr menu or with
ctrl-\e, gives the terminal back and leaves the desktop and everything in
its windows running.
.B desktop \-r
in any terminal comes back to it. Without
.BR \-r ,
a desktop already running under the same session name is left alone and
the command says so.
.PP
Restart Desktop, on the hibr menu, starts the desktop again on whatever
hibr is installed now and keeps the programs running in its terminal
windows. Since 0.99.166 it also upgrades the session's own server when that
is older than the hibr on disk.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-resume
Reattach to the desktop if it is already running, or start one if it is
not.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-session " \fIname\fR"
Which held session this is, for keeping more than one desktop side by side.
The default is
.BR desktop .
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-join
Attach beside an already running desktop as a further display, instead of
starting or resuming one. The desktop then spans both terminals.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-standby
Wait to be joined, now and again each time this display is let go.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name " \fIname\fR"
Name this display, as the Control Panel's Displays pane and
.B desktop ctl displays
show it. Without it a display is
.BI client\- fd\fR.
.SH KEYS
These are the defaults. Each one can be changed in Control Panel >
Keyboard > Shortcuts. Every other key goes to the focused window, so a
program in a terminal window gets its own ctrl-c.
.TP
.BR F10 " or " escape
Open the menu bar. Arrows and letters then work its menus.
.TP
.B alt-tab
Go to the next window.
.TP
.B ctrl-w
Close the focused window.
.TP
.B ctrl-\e
Detach.
.PP
Quit, on the hibr menu, ends the desktop and its windows. A window is
moved by its title bar, or by alt and a drag anywhere in it.
.SH CONTROL
.B desktop ctl
asks a running desktop something through its control socket, or has it do
something, and prints the line of JSON it answers. The desktop is the
session given by
.BR \-\-session ,
else the held session the shell runs in, else
.BR desktop .
.B \-\-socket
names the socket itself, and
.B \-j
sends a request written as JSON as it is. The requests:
.TP
.B displays
The displays attached: each one's name, place and size, whether it is the
primary, and what it draws.
.TP
.B windows
The windows: id, title, place, size, workspace, and whether each has focus.
.TP
.B session
The session's name, whether it is held, the focused window, the current
workspace and how many there are, and the screen's size.
.TP
.B rescan
Read the application folders again, as the catalogue asks after it installs
or removes something.
.TP
.B state
What the desktop waits on: its process id, whether the screen saver or the
lock is up, each watcher, and the descriptors the console wakes for.
.TP
.BR "stats " [ reset | stop ]
What the desktop has done since the last reset, counted the same way on
every platform:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
its own CPU time and that of the programs it ran;
.IP \(bu 2
the wakes, frames and events since;
.IP \(bu 2
how often the sound, network and Bluetooth layers re-read their servers;
.IP \(bu 2
while timing is on, from a reset until a stop, each ticker's runs and
milliseconds.
.RE
.IP
It does not count hold's server or the terminal, which are other processes.
.TP
.BI "move " "id display"
Move a window to a display, named as
.B displays
names it.
.TP
.BI "resize " "id height width"
Resize a window, in cells.
.TP
.BI "focus " id
Raise a window and give it focus.
.B raise
is the same.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B desktop ctl
exits 0 when the desktop answered
.BR {"ok":true} ,
1 for an error the desktop gave, and 2 when there is no desktop to ask or
the request was not understood.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/hibr/desktop.hibr
The settings, written as the Control Panel changes them. It is a hibr
script of its own, read at each start.
.TP
.I ~/.config/hibr/
Also where applications, themes and the rest are found first, before the
system's and the shipped copies.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/hibr/desktop.log
What went wrong, and what the desktop did that is worth knowing, one line
each with the local time. It becomes
.I desktop.log.1
when it grows too large.
.TP
.IB ${TMPDIR:-/tmp} /hibr\-ctl\- uid / session .ctl
The control socket, reachable only by its owner.
.TP
.I @DESKDIR@
The desktop as installed:
.IR session.hibr ,
the window manager, and what ships with it.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HIBR_HOLD
Set inside a held session to that session's name. A desktop started from a
terminal window of another desktop compares it with its own session's name,
and so is held on its own.
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " XDG_STATE_HOME
Where the settings and the log are kept, as above.
.PP
A session file may set any of the desktop's own
.B DT_
settings; they are listed in
.IR programs/desktop/README.md .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hibr (1)
.PP
The desktop's own guide is
.I docs/desktop.md
in hibr's source.
